1
00:00:01,248 --> 00:00:02,137
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:02,643 --> 00:00:07,404
My name is Mary Sirisha Duggyampudi,
and I am a senior software engineer.

3
00:00:07,778 --> 00:00:09,372
Thank you for joining me today.

4
00:00:10,258 --> 00:00:15,893
In this session, I'm going to talk about
AI-driven sensitive data discovery and

5
00:00:15,925 --> 00:00:20,797
how we can integrate with DevSecOps
and privacy compliance practices.

6
00:00:22,365 --> 00:00:27,500
Organizations today are generating
and moving enormous amounts of

7
00:00:27,504 --> 00:00:33,638
data across cloud platforms,
databases, APIs, microservices,

8
00:00:34,022 --> 00:00:36,651
logs, and hybrid environments.

9
00:00:37,383 --> 00:00:41,752
The challenge is that sensitive
data moves just as quickly.

10
00:00:43,264 --> 00:00:48,702
Before we can protect sensitive
data, apply security controls, or

11
00:00:48,827 --> 00:00:53,303
demonstrate compliance, we first
need to answer some basic questions.

12
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:56,202
What sensitive data do we have?

13
00:00:56,858 --> 00:00:58,252
Where is it located?

14
00:00:58,805 --> 00:00:59,957
Where is it moving?

15
00:01:00,550 --> 00:01:01,596
Who owns it?

16
00:01:02,427 --> 00:01:05,895
And are the appropriate
controls actually working?

17
00:01:07,555 --> 00:01:12,904
I will walk through an engineering
approach that combines metadata

18
00:01:12,929 --> 00:01:18,208
intelligence, deterministic rules, AI
and machine learning classification,

19
00:01:18,841 --> 00:01:24,085
runtime telemetry, automated
governance, and CI/CD integration.

20
00:01:24,750 --> 00:01:30,359
Most importantly, the objective is
not simply to discover sensitive data.

21
00:01:31,109 --> 00:01:36,683
It's to turn discovery into
actionable governance and remediation.

22
00:01:38,241 --> 00:01:40,980
Let's start with the fundamental problem.

23
00:01:41,752 --> 00:01:46,845
Sensitive data is no longer
located in one control database.

24
00:01:47,956 --> 00:01:54,842
It can exist across cloud services such
as object storage, managed databases,

25
00:01:55,238 --> 00:01:58,401
analytics platforms, and the SaaS APIs.

26
00:01:59,411 --> 00:02:04,367
It also exists in traditional
operational databases where schema

27
00:02:04,370 --> 00:02:09,094
changes, migrations, or temporary
tables can introduce sensitive

28
00:02:09,095 --> 00:02:13,338
information without going through
the original classification process.

29
00:02:14,046 --> 00:02:18,853
Another area that is frequently
overlooked is logs and telemetry.

30
00:02:19,881 --> 00:02:26,114
Developers may unintentionally log
customer information, authentication

31
00:02:26,115 --> 00:02:29,035
information, or other sensitive values.

32
00:02:29,848 --> 00:02:34,811
Once that happens, the information
can flow into observability

33
00:02:34,848 --> 00:02:36,323
and analytics platforms.

34
00:02:37,070 --> 00:02:40,874
And many enterprises still
operate hybrid infrastructure.

35
00:02:41,396 --> 00:02:47,848
Some applications are cloud native,
while other systems remain on premises

36
00:02:48,184 --> 00:02:50,695
or behind protected enterprise networks.

37
00:02:51,365 --> 00:02:55,560
So the problem is not simply
finding sensitive information once.

38
00:02:56,269 --> 00:02:59,269
The environment itself
is continuously changing.

39
00:03:00,310 --> 00:03:06,665
That means periodic compliance reviews
and manual in-inventories cannot keep

40
00:03:06,739 --> 00:03:09,764
pace with the velocity of modern systems.

41
00:03:11,129 --> 00:03:16,618
And that brings us to why traditional
manual discovery approaches begin to fail.

42
00:03:18,458 --> 00:03:22,405
There are two major problems
here: visibility and ownership.

43
00:03:23,369 --> 00:03:25,005
First is the visibility gap.

44
00:03:25,766 --> 00:03:29,899
A traditional audit gives us
a snapshot of the environment

45
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:32,302
at a particular point in time.

46
00:03:33,132 --> 00:03:38,241
But modern infrastructure can
change within hours or even minutes.

47
00:03:39,057 --> 00:03:41,430
Containers are created and destroyed.

48
00:03:42,173 --> 00:03:44,901
Developers create temporary data sets.

49
00:03:45,304 --> 00:03:47,384
New microservices are deployed.

50
00:03:47,742 --> 00:03:50,178
Schema changes happen continuously.

51
00:03:51,053 --> 00:03:57,010
So there can be a significant difference
between what the compliance inventory

52
00:03:57,415 --> 00:04:00,918
says exists and what actually exists.

53
00:04:02,049 --> 00:04:04,134
The second problem is ownership.

54
00:04:05,563 --> 00:04:11,171
In distributed systems, there
usually is not one team that

55
00:04:11,218 --> 00:04:13,808
understands the entire data landscape.

56
00:04:14,505 --> 00:04:21,356
Sensitive information may appear in
databases, caches, queues, feature

57
00:04:21,361 --> 00:04:24,786
stores, APIs, or third-party integrations.

58
00:04:25,805 --> 00:04:33,238
The security team may identi- identify
the risk, but the application or data team

59
00:04:33,272 --> 00:04:36,203
usually understands the business context.

60
00:04:37,374 --> 00:04:44,113
Therefore, a scalable discovery requires
both continuous technical visibility

61
00:04:44,405 --> 00:04:51,899
and relationship-- reliable ownership
information To address these problems,

62
00:04:51,950 --> 00:04:54,688
I use a layered discovery approach.

63
00:04:55,104 --> 00:05:00,119
Rather than depending on one scanner
or one classification technique, we

64
00:05:00,121 --> 00:05:02,997
combine multiple independent signals.

65
00:05:03,622 --> 00:05:10,017
The first layer is metadata intelligence,
understanding schemas, columns, lineage,

66
00:05:10,296 --> 00:05:13,388
catalog information, and access patterns.

67
00:05:14,121 --> 00:05:18,973
The second is semantic analysis and
machine learning classification,

68
00:05:19,723 --> 00:05:23,486
where we determine what the
data actually represents.

69
00:05:24,128 --> 00:05:30,219
The third is runtime telemetry,
which gives us visibility into how

70
00:05:30,272 --> 00:05:35,991
data is moving and being accessed
during actual system operation.

71
00:05:36,707 --> 00:05:41,770
And I like to think of governance
and remediation as the operational

72
00:05:41,778 --> 00:05:46,704
layer that closes the loop, taking
the findings generated by these

73
00:05:47,318 --> 00:05:49,810
si-signals and turning them into action.

74
00:05:50,485 --> 00:05:56,643
The important architectural principle is
that no single signal needs to be perfect.

75
00:05:57,066 --> 00:06:01,994
When multiple independent signals
agree, we can produce a much

76
00:06:01,995 --> 00:06:03,964
higher confidence classification.

77
00:06:04,965 --> 00:06:09,632
Here, the first layer is metadata
intelligence, and the key idea

78
00:06:09,642 --> 00:06:11,996
here is signal before sampling.

79
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:16,928
Scanning every record in every
enterprise database would be

80
00:06:17,135 --> 00:06:21,803
expensive and potentially introduce
security and performance concerns.

81
00:06:22,447 --> 00:06:28,853
Instead, we can start with metadata
We are considering column names and

82
00:06:28,902 --> 00:06:36,760
types, schema fingerprints, data catalog
tags, data lineage, table lineage,

83
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:40,113
access patterns, and retention labels.

84
00:06:41,052 --> 00:06:45,000
Suppose we find a column
called customer SSN.

85
00:06:45,983 --> 00:06:51,863
We already have a strong signal that the
column could contain sensitive information

86
00:06:51,884 --> 00:06:53,723
without reading millions of rows.

87
00:06:54,553 --> 00:06:59,936
Schema fingerprinting can also
identify structures that reassemble

88
00:07:00,033 --> 00:07:02,681
previously classified sensitive schemas.

89
00:07:03,906 --> 00:07:06,864
Lineage gives us another important signal.

90
00:07:07,628 --> 00:07:13,348
If a source table contains regulated
information and another table is

91
00:07:13,384 --> 00:07:17,681
derived from that source, we should
investigate whether the derived

92
00:07:17,737 --> 00:07:21,143
asset inherited the same sensitivity.

93
00:07:22,209 --> 00:07:28,123
Access patterns can provide add another
clue by showing previously unknown

94
00:07:28,123 --> 00:07:30,366
stores receiving sensitive traffic.

95
00:07:31,397 --> 00:07:36,715
Metadata therefore provides a relatively
low-cost way to determine where

96
00:07:36,812 --> 00:07:38,895
deeper scanning should happen first.

97
00:07:40,232 --> 00:07:46,451
Once we have identified candidate assets,
the next challenge is classification.

98
00:07:47,346 --> 00:07:52,774
I recommend a hybrid approach consisting
of deterministic rules, machine learning

99
00:07:52,774 --> 00:07:55,784
classifiers, and NLP entity detection.

100
00:07:56,530 --> 00:08:00,022
Deterministic rules are very
effective when the information

101
00:08:00,033 --> 00:08:01,321
has a known structure.

102
00:08:02,086 --> 00:08:06,957
Regex patterns and validation
algorithms can detect things such

103
00:08:06,957 --> 00:08:13,594
as Social Security numbers, payment
card numbers, IBANs They are precise,

104
00:08:13,842 --> 00:08:19,112
but they become less effective when
information is masked, encoded,

105
00:08:19,574 --> 00:08:22,341
or represented in unexpected ways.

106
00:08:23,375 --> 00:08:26,058
That's where machine
learning becomes valuable.

107
00:08:27,568 --> 00:08:30,696
ML classifiers can evaluate context.

108
00:08:31,461 --> 00:08:37,260
Instead of only asking whether a value
matches a pattern, the classifier can

109
00:08:37,280 --> 00:08:43,572
consider metadata and the semantic
relationships d- to determine what

110
00:08:43,586 --> 00:08:45,781
the field probably represents.

111
00:08:46,887 --> 00:08:49,651
Confidence scores then become important.

112
00:08:50,354 --> 00:08:56,679
A 99% confidence classification can
potentially follow a different workflow

113
00:08:57,034 --> 00:08:59,703
from a 65% confidence classification.

114
00:09:00,839 --> 00:09:05,751
NLP expands this capability
into unstructured information.

115
00:09:07,121 --> 00:09:13,109
Think about support tickets, comments,
free text fields, or JSON documents.

116
00:09:13,690 --> 00:09:19,277
Sensitive information can exist inside
text where traditional structured

117
00:09:19,284 --> 00:09:21,496
data classifiers are not enough.

118
00:09:22,262 --> 00:09:25,806
This should not be an AI
versus rules decision.

119
00:09:26,481 --> 00:09:31,631
The strongest enterprise approach
is a hybrid deterministic precision

120
00:09:32,156 --> 00:09:39,407
combined with ML context and NLP
under- The previous layers tell us a

121
00:09:39,417 --> 00:09:45,115
lot about stored data, but sensitive
information also exists in motion.

122
00:09:46,332 --> 00:09:50,876
Static scanning alone cannot
tell us everything that happens

123
00:09:51,098 --> 00:09:53,570
while applications are operating.

124
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:57,918
Runtime telemetry helps close this gap.

125
00:09:59,554 --> 00:10:04,816
API trace analysis can help
identify sensitive fields moving

126
00:10:04,881 --> 00:10:06,924
across service boundaries.

127
00:10:07,905 --> 00:10:11,540
Database query logs provide
another important signal.

128
00:10:12,525 --> 00:10:18,345
They can reveal who is accessing
sensitive columns and potentially identify

129
00:10:18,461 --> 00:10:21,228
unexpected or shadow access paths.

130
00:10:22,202 --> 00:10:26,001
And application logs should
also be continuously validated.

131
00:10:27,011 --> 00:10:32,043
Imagine that an application correctly
masks customer information today.

132
00:10:32,629 --> 00:10:36,686
A dependency or configuration
change is deployed tomorrow and

133
00:10:36,732 --> 00:10:38,911
accidentally disables that masking.

134
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:42,904
Runtime validation can
detect that regression.

135
00:10:43,877 --> 00:10:51,234
So the objective is to understand not
only where sensitive data is stored, but

136
00:10:51,280 --> 00:10:54,171
how is actually moves through the system.

137
00:10:56,155 --> 00:10:59,487
Now, the hardest engineering
problem is trust.

138
00:11:00,163 --> 00:11:04,881
Imagine a discovery platform
generating hundreds of alerts every

139
00:11:04,888 --> 00:11:07,280
day and half of them are wrong.

140
00:11:08,202 --> 00:11:10,995
Engineers quickly stop paying attention.

141
00:11:11,530 --> 00:11:15,958
That's why explainability
is not simply an AI feature.

142
00:11:16,760 --> 00:11:19,520
It becomes an a-adoption requirement.

143
00:11:20,729 --> 00:11:26,181
Instead of telling an engineer, "This
column contains PII," we should provide

144
00:11:26,181 --> 00:11:28,097
the evidence behind that decision.

145
00:11:29,651 --> 00:11:35,904
That means what pattern matched, what
metadata contributed, what was the model's

146
00:11:35,929 --> 00:11:39,469
confidence, what evidence was sampled.

147
00:11:40,275 --> 00:11:45,122
Then we can apply different actions
based on confidence and risk.

148
00:11:46,790 --> 00:11:52,339
Very high-confidence findings may
qualify for automated remediation.

149
00:11:53,017 --> 00:11:56,786
Medium-confidence findings
can go through human review.

150
00:11:57,538 --> 00:12:00,733
Human feedback can then
improve the classifier.

151
00:12:01,893 --> 00:12:08,128
That creates a feedback loop, like
detection, then explanation, and go

152
00:12:08,130 --> 00:12:12,779
to human validation, and then model
feedback, and finally improve detection.

153
00:12:13,703 --> 00:12:18,680
That's how we improve accuracy
without losing engineering trust

154
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:20,007
Yeah.

155
00:12:20,730 --> 00:12:23,935
Next one is handling schema
drift and distributed ownership.

156
00:12:24,910 --> 00:12:27,995
This slide shows what
happens when system changes.

157
00:12:29,098 --> 00:12:30,182
Step one, detect.

158
00:12:30,630 --> 00:12:34,860
We identify schema changes from
DDL logs or catalog events.

159
00:12:36,679 --> 00:12:37,978
Step two, classify.

160
00:12:38,202 --> 00:12:42,374
Newly introduced columns automatically
go through classification.

161
00:12:43,625 --> 00:12:44,666
Step three, assign.

162
00:12:45,251 --> 00:12:50,410
Once a finding is identified,
ownership metadata determines which

163
00:12:50,412 --> 00:12:52,470
team is responsible to do that.

164
00:12:53,364 --> 00:12:54,836
Step four, govern.

165
00:12:55,548 --> 00:12:59,877
Policy as a code determines
whether the change satisfies

166
00:13:00,042 --> 00:13:01,684
organizational requirements.

167
00:13:02,535 --> 00:13:06,091
Consider a developer adding
a new date of birth field.

168
00:13:07,188 --> 00:13:13,090
Technically, the application may
work perfectly, but from a privacy

169
00:13:13,090 --> 00:13:18,427
perspective, the risk profile of
that data set cha-- just changed.

170
00:13:19,308 --> 00:13:24,218
Instead of discovering that
months later during an audit, this

171
00:13:24,218 --> 00:13:30,337
architecture allows us to identify
the change close to when it happens.

172
00:13:32,108 --> 00:13:36,221
Discovery has a limited value
unless it drives action.

173
00:13:37,820 --> 00:13:40,213
This is where we can close the loop.

174
00:13:41,087 --> 00:13:48,230
When a sensitive data finding occurs,
we can automatically create either

175
00:13:48,233 --> 00:13:53,656
Jira or GitHub issue containing
the evidence and remediation SLA.

176
00:13:54,744 --> 00:14:01,063
Policy engines such as OPA or Signal can
enforce requirements before deployment.

177
00:14:01,918 --> 00:14:06,685
Vault or KMS integrations can help
validate encryption requirements.

178
00:14:07,407 --> 00:14:13,303
And the same platform can
generate evidence packages

179
00:14:13,608 --> 00:14:15,117
for compliance activities.

180
00:14:15,959 --> 00:14:18,786
The workflow can also depend on severity.

181
00:14:19,881 --> 00:14:24,196
A high severity finding
might create an incident.

182
00:14:25,308 --> 00:14:31,943
New PII introduced through a schema change
might generate a pull request comment

183
00:14:32,422 --> 00:14:34,829
and prevent promotion until reviewed.

184
00:14:36,185 --> 00:14:43,411
An unencrypted sensitive store could
initiate an encryption-related workflow.

185
00:14:44,306 --> 00:14:50,541
These changes since new data discovery
from a passive cataloging exercise

186
00:14:50,603 --> 00:14:53,560
into an active governance system.

187
00:14:55,107 --> 00:14:58,752
Now we are bringing the concept
directly into DevSecOps.

188
00:14:59,529 --> 00:15:01,657
There are four opportunities to control.

189
00:15:01,887 --> 00:15:04,032
First, pre-commit hooks.

190
00:15:05,252 --> 00:15:10,600
These can identify hard-coded secrets or
potentially sensitive schema additions

191
00:15:11,097 --> 00:15:14,007
before code even enters the repository.

192
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:19,831
Second, during a pull request,
schema differences can trigger

193
00:15:20,241 --> 00:15:24,146
classification and provide
feedback directly to the developer.

194
00:15:25,494 --> 00:15:27,901
Third, deployment gates.

195
00:15:29,108 --> 00:15:32,131
It can enforce organizational policies.

196
00:15:32,665 --> 00:15:38,574
For example, a sensitive data store
may require classification, encryption

197
00:15:38,574 --> 00:15:43,431
attestation, and an appropriate
retention label before promotion.

198
00:15:45,209 --> 00:15:53,019
Finally, we need post-deployment
drift detection because production

199
00:15:53,040 --> 00:15:54,948
can change after deployment.

200
00:15:56,026 --> 00:16:02,011
This creates a continuous life
cycle like develop, detect, then

201
00:16:02,012 --> 00:16:08,367
validate, and then govern, and then
deploy, and final one is monitor.

202
00:16:09,481 --> 00:16:15,876
The advantage is that privacy and
security become part of the engineering

203
00:16:15,881 --> 00:16:22,262
workflow instead of an external process
developers encounter at the end.

204
00:16:23,156 --> 00:16:26,626
Another important concept
is that not all sensitive

205
00:16:26,626 --> 00:16:28,637
information represents equal risk.

206
00:16:29,481 --> 00:16:33,824
A system should therefore move
beyond sample classification

207
00:16:34,074 --> 00:16:36,444
toward risk-stratified coverage.

208
00:16:37,386 --> 00:16:42,120
The workflow starts with a baseline
inventory, continuous scanning

209
00:16:42,123 --> 00:16:46,731
as a system change, calculates
risk and generates evidence.

210
00:16:47,669 --> 00:16:52,609
Different regulatory context can
also influence prioritization.

211
00:16:53,588 --> 00:16:58,990
GDPR special category information,
HIPAA protected health information, and

212
00:16:58,997 --> 00:17:04,260
PCI DSS cardho-cardholder information
can carry different obligations.

213
00:17:05,368 --> 00:17:10,385
Instead of giving every finding
the same priority, we focus

214
00:17:10,424 --> 00:17:15,671
remediation effort where exposure
and regulatory impact are greatest.

215
00:17:16,512 --> 00:17:21,310
And importantly, automation
does not automatically make

216
00:17:21,312 --> 00:17:23,702
a, an organization compliant.

217
00:17:24,390 --> 00:17:30,587
What it provides is continuous visibility,
control evidence, and repeatable

218
00:17:30,609 --> 00:17:33,707
process that can support compliance.

219
00:17:34,668 --> 00:17:37,976
There are three engineering
trade-offs I want to emphasize.

220
00:17:38,543 --> 00:17:41,090
The first is coverage versus cost.

221
00:17:41,686 --> 00:17:46,000
Full content scanning provides
deep coverage but can be expensive.

222
00:17:46,801 --> 00:17:52,706
Metadata-first triage helps us prioritize
the assets requiring deeper analysis.

223
00:17:54,060 --> 00:17:56,254
Second is precision versus recall.

224
00:17:57,436 --> 00:18:03,609
If we tune too aggressively for precision,
we may miss sensitive information.

225
00:18:04,265 --> 00:18:08,938
In highly regulated environments,
we may intentionally favor a

226
00:18:08,948 --> 00:18:13,927
recall and use efficient human
review for ambiguous findings.

227
00:18:15,056 --> 00:18:17,976
Third is automation versus explainability.

228
00:18:19,216 --> 00:18:25,241
Automated remediation is powerful, but
it should require sufficient confidence.

229
00:18:25,928 --> 00:18:30,797
Engineers are much more likely
to remediate a finding when they

230
00:18:30,854 --> 00:18:34,004
understand why the system generated it.

231
00:18:35,969 --> 00:18:42,573
So the successful sensitive data discovery
is not about maximizing one metric.

232
00:18:42,929 --> 00:18:48,456
It's about finding the appropriate
balance among coverage, cost,

233
00:18:48,622 --> 00:18:51,819
accuracy, automation, and trust.

234
00:18:52,706 --> 00:18:56,295
If you want to take something
practical from this session back

235
00:18:56,304 --> 00:19:00,611
to your organization, I would
focus on four reusable patterns.

236
00:19:01,073 --> 00:19:03,793
First is discovery pipeline blueprint.

237
00:19:04,542 --> 00:19:10,422
Combine metadata, semantic analysis,
and runtime telemetry instead of

238
00:19:10,472 --> 00:19:12,680
depending on one detection technique.

239
00:19:13,529 --> 00:19:20,105
Second is policy as code Convert
privacy and security requirements

240
00:19:20,261 --> 00:19:24,410
into controls that can execute
within delivery pipelines.

241
00:19:25,684 --> 00:19:27,411
Third is evidence automation.

242
00:19:28,517 --> 00:19:32,575
Do not wait until audit time
to reconstruct what happened.

243
00:19:33,566 --> 00:19:37,964
Capture classifications,
approvals, remediation, and

244
00:19:37,984 --> 00:19:39,845
control evidence continuously.

245
00:19:40,779 --> 00:19:43,531
And fourth is human-in-the-loop feedback.

246
00:19:44,460 --> 00:19:50,047
Domain teams know their data better
than a centralized model ever will.

247
00:19:51,107 --> 00:19:55,353
Their feedback should continuously
improve classification quality.

248
00:19:56,472 --> 00:20:01,995
These patterns are reusable because
they are architectural concepts

249
00:20:02,127 --> 00:20:08,193
rather than requirements tied to one
particular application or technology.

250
00:20:08,997 --> 00:20:15,136
So where we are heading, the goal is not
simply a scanner that tells us whether

251
00:20:15,252 --> 00:20:17,431
a field contains sensitive information.

252
00:20:18,057 --> 00:20:24,600
The goal is an intelligent governance
system that continuously understands what

253
00:20:24,616 --> 00:20:32,112
sensitive data exists, where it exists,
where it flows, who owns it, and whether

254
00:20:32,277 --> 00:20:34,423
the appropriate controls are functioning.

255
00:20:35,654 --> 00:20:41,352
That gives us continuous visibility
rather than an inventory that's

256
00:20:41,355 --> 00:20:43,800
updated only during audits.

257
00:20:44,631 --> 00:20:51,639
It also improves operational resilience
because governance failures can appear as

258
00:20:51,722 --> 00:20:57,312
operational signals instead of becoming
compliance findings months later.

259
00:20:58,423 --> 00:21:02,553
And finally, these controls
need to remain engineer-native.

260
00:21:03,387 --> 00:21:09,307
Security and privacy should not be
bolt-on processes that developers

261
00:21:09,328 --> 00:21:12,583
have m-- have to navigate separately.

262
00:21:13,553 --> 00:21:17,783
They should become part of the
same pipelines engineers already

263
00:21:17,830 --> 00:21:20,043
use to build and deploy software.

264
00:21:20,801 --> 00:21:25,461
So ultimately, the progression
is discover, first discover,

265
00:21:25,960 --> 00:21:32,014
understand it properly, and then
assess the risk and govern it and

266
00:21:32,276 --> 00:21:37,490
need to remediate, and finally,
continuously monitor what is happening.

267
00:21:39,227 --> 00:21:45,147
That is how sensitive data discovery
evolves from a compliance activity

268
00:21:45,235 --> 00:21:48,087
into an engineering capability.

269
00:21:49,209 --> 00:21:54,310
To summarize, sensitive data discovery
needs to become continuous because

270
00:21:54,342 --> 00:21:56,243
modern systems are continuous.

271
00:21:56,867 --> 00:22:01,637
By combining metadata intelligence, AI
and machine learning classification,

272
00:22:01,901 --> 00:22:08,262
runtime telemetry, automated governance,
and DevSecOps integration, organizations

273
00:22:08,476 --> 00:22:13,932
can identify sensitive data earlier and
connect those discoveries directly to

274
00:22:13,986 --> 00:22:16,334
actionable security and privacy controls.

275
00:22:17,149 --> 00:22:22,403
The takeaway, the key takeaway I
would leave you with this is finding

276
00:22:22,403 --> 00:22:24,833
sensitive data is only the beginning.

277
00:22:25,318 --> 00:22:30,707
The real value becomes-- The real
value comes from understanding it,

278
00:22:31,066 --> 00:22:35,150
assigning ownership, and tuning
that intelligence into action.

279
00:22:36,079 --> 00:22:37,292
Thank you for your time.

280
00:22:37,447 --> 00:22:39,792
I hope you find the session valuable.

